ines \\(L_{t}\\) by additional non‑sunny lines that are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   For any integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n-3\\) we may select \\(s\\) distinct integers \\(t_{1},\\dots ,t_{s}\\) from \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and replace the corresponding lines \\(L_{t_{i}}\\) by the vertical lines \\(x=t_{i}\\).  \n   Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving distinctness of the lines and the covering condition (the points \\((t_{i},b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) are already covered by the vertical line \\(x=t_{i}\\)).  \n\n   Consequently, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can choose exactly \\(k\\) of the lines among the set \\(\\{L_{t}\\}\\) to keep sunny and replace the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) of them by vertical lines. Together with the three original non‑sunny lines we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all required lattice points and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) in the whole range \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), all these values are admissible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nDefine the three *non‑sunny* lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}\\colon x=1,\\qquad \nL_{2}\\colon y=1,\\qquad \nL_{3}\\colon x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach of them is respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines in (1).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b=n+1\\) is impossible, so at least one of the equalities \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) must hold. Hence the point belongs to \\(L_{1}\\) or \\(L_{2}\\). ∎\n\nThus the three lines (1) already satisfy the cov